But dirichlet boundaries would not allow a large values close to the coastline, which may or may not be realistic. Neumann boundaries should still be sensible; if your coastline is very irregular however, the increase in variance may be problematic, and that is perhaps what you're seeing.  Having a mesh that extends inland should not be a problem, you just need to exclude those regions from the further analysis, by forcibly setting them to zero, and with zero variance, in the inla output.
If you're using the excursions package to calculate the excursion sets, there is also an option to specify only a subset of the model to operate on.

