On Tue, 2015-05-19 at 20:05 +0200, Virgilio Gómez Rubio wrote:
> Hi,
>
>
> I am trying to fit a model that uses a multivariate prior but I am not
> sure that I can do it with R-INLA. Say that the model is a standard
> linear regression with two covariates and that I want to put a
> bivariate prior on the coefficients. The prior I want to use is not
> implemented in R-INLA. What would it be the best way to include such a
> prior?
>
>
> I have noticed the "expression:" and "table:" priors but I do not this
> these are suitable to define a multivariate prior.
>
>
> I think that I could use the same approach as in [1] where we fixed
> the values of the model parameters and then fit some conditional
> models with a prior of our choice and combine then using Bayesian
> Model Averaging, but I am wondering whether there is a simpler
> approach.

Hi,

yes, this is a design issue... its not easy to mix in a multivate prior.
are you thinking of using a g-prior ?

H

--
Håvard Rue
he...@r-inla.org

