Havard and Elias,

Thank you for your kind and instant reply. I know how to set prediction in INLA and have tried. Elias, my data is quite similar to yours and it is very helpful. In terms of extrapolation (i.e., forecast) of count data, especially for disease data, it seems that there are still some problems. In your data, for example, you make some prediction of number of deaths in 2009 and calculated the corresponding MSE, still using the expected number of death in 2009 (which is indeed available in your database).  I'm wondering what we can forecast in 2010, using the best predictive model, the number of death (if this is the case, there is no expected number of death available, right?) or the SMR?  the latter would be of great concern for a epidemiologist I think. Looking forward to your response. Thank you.

Yi

On Sat, May 30, 2015 at 6:00 PM, Elias T Krainski <eliask...@gmail.com> wrote:

> On 30/05/15 09:20, Håvard Rue wrote:
> > you have to build the forcast into your model, using NA as the response.
>
> There is an example in the material I used for a recent INLA course at
>  http://www.math.ntnu.no/~eliask/br2015/
> The file r/cwb-select.R runs 864 models including 5 (five) space-time models type. The forecast is done for one year ahead (the outcome for 2009 is set as NA in the example) and one year before (there is no outcome observed for 1995).
>
> In other work I and a colleague tried to evaluate models for 2 steps ahead. Our experience is that random effects (like dynamic intercepts) are not good to forecast as to fit the data. In the example above you can see that the best models for forecast (low mse) includes parametric trends instead of complex random effects and the better fitting (low DIC and LCPO) includes complex random effects.
>
> Elias
